{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-122314",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/assets/efdb69673b4088d3c021d78282642ff7abc57158e23704cd3d5e7ff2559cbdbb.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=3x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3x.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x| & =t+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x| & =3t+3K \\\\ x & ={e}^{3t+3K} \\\\ x & =C{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=2$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=2,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=2{e}^{3t}.\n$$",
